<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    The LOAD-TIME format-string resolver (`option formatstring off|static|full`,
    default `static`).

    The XML wrapper only provides the harness architecture. Every pass clears it
    and loads the same in-tree ELF fixture through the CLI's own loader; it is
    `int main(int argc, char **argv) { printf("%d %s\n", argc, argv[0]); }`
    built for x86-64, so `main` reaches `printf@plt` with the format pointer in
    RDI as a `lea rax,[rip+0xea8]` / `mov rdi,rax` pair.

    Pass 1 (`formatstring off`) reproduces the defect: nothing says what the
    variadic arguments are, so the `%d` argument is widened to the register's
    full width and the `%s` argument keeps the element type of the pointer it
    was loaded through.

    Pass 2 (the shipped default) is the load-time resolver. The Listing supplies
    the call edge, the compiler spec supplies RDI as the format parameter's
    storage, and a constant-fold over the call's own basic block supplies
    0x402004 - so `"%d %s\n"` is read out of the image and the per-call-site
    override is parked BEFORE `main` is decompiled. The arguments render `int`
    and `char *`, at one decompile.

    Pass 3 is `full`, which is the ported Ghidra `FormatStringAnalyzer` loop:
    decompile, read the constant off the lifted CALL, install the override,
    decompile again. It reaches the same answer WITHOUT the Listing, which is
    what the extra decompile buys and why the value is kept.

    Passes 4 to 6 (off, the default, full) load fmtjoin_x86_64 (source beside
    it, gcc -O2) and pin two call sites whose load-time answer the drive has to
    overrule. All three passes must render the same calls.

    report: the format printf receives is picked on a jump table. The case
    bodies are reached only through the table and jump back to the join that
    the default path falls into, so the window before the call sees only the
    default path's "default %ld\n". Taking that answer closes the call to one
    vararg, and the %s argument and the parameter it came from disappear. The
    drive sees the join, the format there is not the resolved constant, and the
    parked override is withdrawn.

    show: an alloca frame. The drive cannot track the stack pointer, so a
    closed prototype picks up the slot the call pushes its return address into
    as one more argument. The call then passes more arguments than the override
    declares, and the parked override is withdrawn.

    The override closes the prototype, so the compiler spec places each
    conversion where it would place a NAMED argument. Passes 7 to 14 pin the
    targets and strings where that is not where the call put it; on each the
    default must render what off renders.

    Passes 7 to 9 (off, the default, full) load macho_imports_arm64, an Apple
    arm64 image. Apple passes every vararg on the stack, so a closed prototype
    reads the %d from w1: _main grew a phantom parameter and printed it instead
    of a0 * 3 + 7. No Apple AArch64 site is typed.

    Passes 10 to 12 (off, the default, full) load fmtabi_armhf (gcc -O2 -marm,
    hard-float). A %f vararg travels in r2:r3 and a named double in d0, so a
    closed prototype read d0 and d1: f_conv lost its int parameter and printed
    an uninitialized register, f_sum printed (a, b) instead of (a + b, a). ARM
    declines a floating conversion, and still types the integer site f_is.

    Passes 13 and 14 (off, the default) load fmtedge_x86_64. show passes a
    format that lives in writable .data and is rewritten to "v=%s" before the
    call, so the image's "v=%d" typed the pointer argument int. A string
    outside a read-only section is not read. wide prints %lc, a wint_t: it is
    typed as an int-sized unsigned value, not a char, so no truncating cast
    appears.

    Passes 15 to 20 (off, the default, full on each) load fmtlf_x86_64 and
    fmtlf_armhf, one source built for both (gcc -O2; -marm hard-float). The l
    in %lf changes nothing for printf and asks scanf for a double *. Typed as
    an unsigned long, show lost the double from xmm0 and grew two phantom
    parameters, and rd's double local became an integer (on ARM an int array).
    x86-64 now passes the double and the int; ARM declines the floating printf
    conversions, so show and show2 render what off renders, while the scanf
    pointer is still typed and drops off's phantom trailing argument.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
